            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.07,
                0.312,
                0.184,
                0.323
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "joann.kim@JPM.com",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.07,
                0.338,
                0.252,
                0.354
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "** Sales Commentary only",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.067,
                0.367,
                0.919,
                0.53
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Overall Day 1 feedback from the summit points to a still cautious but selectively constructive stance on China. Most investors remain underweight China at a top-down level, citing weak fundamentals, lack of broad-based macro recovery, and lingering skepticism around property—with the sustainability of property recovery still a question mark—though there is growing recognition that policy support is incrementally improving and downside risks may be better priced. Interest is highly selective, with strong engagement around AI and semiconductors (particularly equipment and domestic supply chains), industrials and automation, energy transition, and power infrastructure. Investors highlighted names such as AMEC in tech equipment, and industrial leaders including Sany Heavy, Weichai, Techtronic, and Minth. There was continued interest in power equipment and materials while views on EVs and batteries are turning more mixed amid margin pressure and lithium price concerns. Property is seen as stabilizing only in select Tier 1 locations, rather than a durable recovery theme. Most investors' interest remains on high-quality industrial and tech leaders while closely monitoring geopolitical uncertainty and policy-driven market moves. Stay tuned for further updates on NEV/ESS company meetings!",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "title",
            "bbox": [
                0.07,
                0.544,
                0.3,
                0.558
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Industrial Innovation - Robotics"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.067,
                0.573,
                0.927,
                0.678
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Our expert session reaffirms that humanoid and embodied AI robots are firmly established as a long-term structural growth theme, though the industry remains in an early, validation-heavy phase. Near-term progress will be driven less by headline model breakthroughs and more by incremental advances in hardware reliability, system architecture (VLA + world models), and high-quality data generation. Commercial value is expected to first emerge in structured industrial settings—such as logistics, manufacturing, and pharmacies—while broader generalization and household adoption are likely to remain longer dated. Over the next 1–3 years, China is positioned to lead real-world deployment as pilots scale and ROI becomes demonstrable, laying the foundation for more meaningful volume growth in the longer term.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.067,
                0.691,
                0.927,
                0.854
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Investor questions focused on understanding key leadership aspects of humanoid robots, including brain (algorithm), hardware and cost leadership, as well as bottlenecks for accelerating adoption. The session with Harmonious Drive Systems (688017 CH) attracted high investor interest, with the company maintaining stable product quality and only a small gap versus Japanese peers, while offering substantial price advantages (\\(\\sim 50\\%\\)) to capture meaningful volumes at top global robotic players such as KUKA, ABB, and Yaskawa. Harmonic drive's orders from leading customers have already exceeded last year's full-year volume, resulting in an order book well above current capacity. Notably, investor questions during the robotics session also centered on the trade-off between harmonic (precision) and planetary (cost competitiveness) drive systems and which components will dominate in commercialization. Many investors also asked about the competitiveness of Korean and Japanese players, including Boston Dynamics and Hyundai Motor Group. Overall, investors remain constructive on humanoid robots with multiple upcoming events including the Teslabot launch & commercialization. (Karen Li - Robotics in Transition : IPO pipelines, margin surprises, and global race for scale)",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.068,
                0.868,
                0.691,
                0.884
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Growth in AI capex is a multi-year thematic - with spillover impacts on the FA industry.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.067,
                0.898,
                0.918,
                0.931
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Interesting to note a constructive tone from Shenzhen Inovance (300124 CH) management, with AI-related demand and domestic substitution driving a multi-year growth opportunity. While near-term margin pressure persists due to cost inflation and",
            "merge_prev": false
        }
